I. Introduction
Regional Price Parity (RPP) is a key economic measure used to compare the cost of goods and services across U.S. states. With a baseline national average of 100, RPP enables comparisons of purchasing power and affordability between regions. Scores above 100 indicate a higher cost of living, while scores below 100 indicate a lower cost. Understanding RPP is vital for evaluating economic well-being, setting wage standards, designing housing policies, and supporting individual relocation or business investment decisions.
This project investigates how RPP scores vary across states and how they relate to income levels and affordability. Through data analysis and visualizations, we aim to identify affordability gaps and explore policy and economic implications.

II. Objectives
The core objectives of this project are to:
Analyze the variation in RPP scores across U.S. states.
Examine the relationship between RPP and median household income.
Identify affordability challenges where high RPP is not matched by proportionally higher income.
Assess how housing costs influence overall RPP.
Provide recommendations for policymakers and individuals based on findings.

